/*Q5. You are working with Java instance variables and need to understand the default values assigned by Java when variables are declared
without explicit initialization.
Create a class named DefaultValues and declare instance variables for the following data types without assigning values:
• byte
• short
• int
• long
• float
• double
• char
• boolean
• String
The DefaultValues class should create an object of its own class and display the default value of each instance variable using that object.
Input Format:
• There is no input required for this program.
Output Format:
• Display the default values in the following format:
byte: <value>
short: <value>
int: <value>
long: <value>
float: <value>
double: <value>
char: [<value>]
boolean: <value>
String: <value>
*/
public class DefaultValues {
    byte b;
    short s;
    int i;
    long l;
    float f;
    double d;
    char c;
    boolean bool;
    String str;
 
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DefaultValues obj = new DefaultValues();
        System.out.println("byte: " + obj.b);
        System.out.println("short: " + obj.s);
        System.out.println("int: " + obj.i);
        System.out.println("long: " + obj.l);
        System.out.println("float: " + obj.f);
        System.out.println("double: " + obj.d);
        System.out.println("char: [" + obj.c + "]");
        System.out.println("boolean: " + obj.bool);
        System.out.println("String: " + obj.str);
    }
}
